WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.800
This is my microscope at home in Qatar.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.920
The photograph was posted on 1 July 2017; the original camera date is not verified.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.967
I bought the instrument in the Qasr El Einy area of Cairo.

00:00:15.967 --> 00:00:20.633
Getting it to Qatar took considerable effort.

00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.920
This journey is remembered, not photographed.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:38.920
I wanted to be able to return to an observation when the question occurred to me, not only during scheduled laboratory hours.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:45.800
University instruments came with protocols and calendars, for good reasons.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:51.920
I valued that training, but curiosity kept its own hours.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:59.300
The photograph shows a vacation morning: books, notes and an American Chemical Society mug.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:05.920
No supervisor was waiting for a result.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:12.800
At home, I could come back to the same object another day.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.920
I could spend time looking, change my mind, and look again.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.967
A microscope lets you see.

00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:28.633
Explanation still needs controls, methods and evidence that other people can examine.

00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:32.920
I had a place to practice the first part.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:39.800
It stood there on my desk, among the ordinary objects of a morning at home.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.920
Scientific attention had somewhere to live between classes.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.967
The instrument came from Cairo.

00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:55.633
The photograph is from Qatar.

00:01:55.633 --> 00:01:59.920
What I remember most is having the time to ask questions that belonged to me.
